{:edb/db-before-t 894 :edb/db-after-t 895 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534207 50 #inst "2026-10-06T10:50:49.246Z" 13194139534207 true] [17592186157070 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{3}{5} \\ln(x+2) +1 \\leq 0$." 13194139534207 false] [17592186157070 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{3}{5} \\ln(x+2) +1 \\leq 0.$" 13194139534207 true] [17592186157070 1145 "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+1 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2) & \\le -1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2) & \\le -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)} & \\le {e}^{-5/3} \\\\ x+2 & \\le {e}^{-5/3} \\\\ x & \\le {e}^{-5/3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+2> 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x > -2\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x \\leq e^{-5/3}-2,$ which can be expressed as $\\left(-\\infty, e^{-5/3}-2\\right].$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x > -2,$ which can be expressed as $(-2, \\infty).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\infty, e^{-5/3}-2\\right] \\cap (-2, \\infty) = \\left(-2,e^{-5/3}-2 \\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in \\left(-2, e^{-5/3}-2\\right],$ which can also be written as $-2<  x \\leq e^{-5/3}-2.$" 13194139534207 true] [17592186157070 1145 "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5}\\ln (x + 2) + 1 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\ln (x + 2) & \\le - 1 \\\\ \\ln (x + 2) & \\le - \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e$. We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}e^{\\ln (x + 2)} & \\le e^{-5/3} \\\\ x + 2 & \\le e^{-5/3} \\\\ x & \\le e^{-5/3} - 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+2> 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x > -2\n$$\n\nOur solution is\n\n$$\nx \\leq e^{-5/3}-2\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $[MATH: \\left(-\\infty, e^{-5/3}-2\\right].]$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x > -2$, which can be expressed as $(-2, \\infty)$. To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$[MATH: \\left(-\\infty, e^{-5/3}-2\\right] \\cap (-2, \\infty) = \\left(-2,e^{-5/3}-2 \\right]]$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $[MATH: x \\in \\left(-2, e^{-5/3}-2\\right],]$ which can also be written as\n\n$$\n-2< x \\leq e^{-5/3}-2\n$$" 13194139534207 false] [17592186157071 1100 "Solve the inequality ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981717/assets/d278633b66c1886aee16cda9c1c05954cdb887ff8ebdc3c9e902d7b20dbeee46.png)" 13194139534207 true] [17592186157071 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\ln(x+2) - 6 < \\ln(x-2)$." 13194139534207 false] [17592186157071 1145 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981717/assets/e413144c7c08fc3fd3ad3293577766c181d40615bf7a8632fa75d71d3ec9cdd6.png) This gives the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981717/assets/364af5a514a3dd6aa3eebd44488f5ac35c8dfcd295e6a21da56a85ae5b83a049.png)\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981717/assets/90ca0aab01a6801eaa007720b8b16a091a0160979d56b7e46b