\\ln (x + 2)} \\cdot e^{-6} & < e^{\\ln (x - 2)} \\\\ (x + 2)e^{-6} & < x - 2 \\\\ (x + 2) & < (x - 2)e^{6} \\\\ (x + 2) & < xe^{6} - 2e^{6} \\\\ 2 + 2e^{6} & < (e^{6} - 1)x \\\\ \\frac{2 + 2e^{6}}{e^{6} - 1} & < x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + 2 & > 0 & ⇒ & x & > - 2 \\\\ x - 2 & > 0 & ⇒ & x & > 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nOur solution is\n\n$$\nx > \\dfrac{2+2e^6}{e^6-1}\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2+2e^6}{e^6-1},\\infty \\right)\n$$\n\nAdditionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x > -2$ and $x>2$, which can be expressed as $(-2,\\infty)$ and $(2,\\infty)$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2+2e^6}{e^6-1},\\infty \\right) \\cap (-2, \\infty) \\cap (2, \\infty) = \\left(\\dfrac{2+2e^6}{e^6-1}, \\infty \\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\in \\left(\\dfrac{2+2e^6}{e^6-1}, \\infty \\right)\n$$\n\nwhich can also be written as\n\n$$\nx > \\dfrac{2+2e^6}{e^6-1}\n$$" 13194139534207 false] [17592186157259 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282330 13194139534207 true] [17592186157259 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282344 13194139534207 true] [17592186157259 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282349 13194139534207 true] [17592186157259 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282356 13194139534207 true] [17592186157259 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282363 13194139534207 true] [17592186157260 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282337 13194139534207 true] [17592186157260 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282370 13194139534207 true] [17592186157260 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282377 13194139534207 true] [17592186157260 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282384 13194139534207 true] [17592186157845 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534207 true] [17592186157845 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\ln (x + 2) - 5 \\ge \\ln (3x)$." 131